[1.625, "o", "\u001b[?2026h\u001b[44;103H\u001b[1K\r\u001b[?2026l"]
[0.024, "o", "\u001b[?2026h\u001b[2;44r\u001b[2;44r\u001b[1;103s\u001b[1;1H\u001b[14S\u001b[2;44r\u001b[s\u001b[30;1H  \u001b[36m@@ -1,17 +1,18 @@\r\n\u001b[39m  \u001b[31m-# Incident 2, step 2.1 -- the readiness probe that passed review.\u001b[39m\u001b[35X\r\n  \u001b[32m+# Incident 2, step 2.4 -- the fix, and three separate lessons in it.\u001b[39m\u001b[32X\r\n   #\u001b[99X\r\n  \u001b[31m-# \"Readiness should verify we can actually read our data.\" Nobody argues with\u001b[39m\u001b[23X\r\n  \u001b[31m-# that in a pull request, and at three replicas nobody notices: a scan every two\u001b[39m\u001b[20X\r\n  \u001b[31m-# seconds, three times over, is a rounding error on the database.\u001b[39m\u001b[35X\r\n  \u001b[32m+# 1. READY_MODE=cached. One background refresher per replica does one cheap\u001b[39m\u001b[25X\r\n  \u001b[32m+#    ping per interval; the probe reads a flag. The cost is O(1) whatever the\u001b[39m\u001b[23X\r\n  \u001b[32m+#    replica count, which is the only property that survives autoscaling.\u001b[39m\u001b[27X\r\n   #\u001b[99X\r\n  \u001b[31m-# The cost is replicas x (1/period). That is the entire incident. Everything that\u001b[39m\u001b[19X\r\n  \u001b[31m-# follows is arithmetic.\u001b[39m\u001b[76X\r\n  \u001b[32m+# 2. The pool is bud"]
[0.0, "o", "geted against the wall instead of ignoring it. 12 workers\u001b[39m\u001b[23X\r\n  \u001b[32m+#    at 3 connec\u001b[39m\u001b[84X\u001b[1;44r\u001b[44;20H\u001b[?2026l\u001b[?2026h\u001b[2;44r\u001b[2;44r\u001b[1;103s\u001b[1;1H\u001b[13S\u001b[2;44r\u001b[s\u001b[31;20H\u001b[32mtions plus 3 api replicas at 3 is 45, against the 54 the\r\n\u001b[39m  \u001b[32m+#    database will actually give us. The number was always knowable.\u001b[39m\u001b[32X\r\n   #\u001b[99X\r\n  \u001b[31m-# The worker is scaled by KEDA on queue depth, from zero. Its pool is four\u001b[39m\u001b[26X\r\n  \u001b[31m-# connections per replica. The database has 60, of which RDS keeps 4 for itself\u001b[39m\u001b[21X\r\n  \u001b[31m-# and 2 more are held back for the observer -- 54 are actually available.\u001b[39m\u001b[27X\r\n  \u001b[31m-# maxReplicaCount is 24 here, so the ceiling is 24x4 = 96 worker connections\u001b[39m\u001b[24X\r\n  \u001b[31m-# plus 3x4 from the api against a budget of 54. The wall is not a risk, it is\u001b[39m\u001b[23X\r\n  \u001b[31m-# a schedule.\u001b[39m\u001b[87X\r\n  \u001b[32m+# 3. maxReplicaCount is 12, not 24. An autoscaler with no ceiling is a way to\u001b[39m\u001b[23X\r\n  \u001b[32m+#    convert an incident into an invoice -- and on this"]
[0.001, "o", " account, into a real\u001b[39m\u001b[24X\r\n  \u001b[32m+#    one. Karpenter stops buying nodes because KEDA stops asking.\u001b[39m\u001b[35X\r\n  \u001b[32m+#\u001b[39m\u001b[99X\r\n  \u001b[32m+# Readiness also gets a longer period. It is no longer expensive, but there \u001b[39m\u001b[24X\u001b[1;44r\u001b[44;80H\u001b[?2026l\u001b[?2026h\u001b[2;44r\u001b[2;44r\u001b[1;103s\u001b[1;1H\u001b[26S\u001b[2;44r\u001b[s\u001b[18;80H\u001b[32mis\r\n\u001b[39m  \u001b[32m+# nothing to gain from asking twice a second either.\u001b[39m\u001b[48X\r\n   apiVersion: apps/v1\u001b[81X\r\n   kind: Deployment\u001b[84X\r\n  \u001b[36m@@ -45,9 +46,9 @@\u001b[39m spec:\u001b[78X\r\n                 value: \"200\"\u001b[74X\r\n               - name: POOL_MAX\u001b[72X\r\n  \u001b[31m-              value: \"4\"\u001b[39m\u001b[76X\r\n  \u001b[32m+              value: \"3\"\u001b[39m\u001b[76X\r\n               - name: HEALTHZ_MODE\u001b[68X\r\n                 value: \"local\"\u001b[72X\r\n               - name: READY_MODE\u001b[70X\r\n  \u001b[31m-              value: \"db_each_call\"\u001b[39m\u001b[65X\r\n  \u001b[32m+              value: \"cached\"\u001b[39m\u001b[71X\r\n               - name: QUEUE_URL\u001b[71X\r\n                 value: ${QUEUE_URL}\u001b[67X\r\n  \u001b[36m@@ -69,5 +70,5 @@\u001b[39m spec:\u001b[78X\r\n             rea"]
